# Ava — staging-slot settings. Template for deploy/.env.staging, which is
# git-ignored like every other .env. You rarely write this by hand:
#
#   cd deploy && ./slot.sh init      # generates it from your working deploy/.env
#   ./slot.sh up -d --build          # then open http://127.0.0.1:8097
#
# THIS FILE MUST BE SELF-CONTAINED. `--env-file` REPLACES deploy/.env rather than
# layering on top of it, so a file carrying only the deltas below will not start —
# compose refuses on the first `${VAR:?}` it cannot interpolate. That is what
# `slot.sh init` is for: it copies your stable settings and then forces the five
# values that MUST differ. Each of those five is a data-corruption or
# silent-clobber bug if it is left equal to stable's.

# ── the five that must differ ────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Own state: SQLite stores, secrets, ava.yaml, and its own HMAC session-signing
# key. Two bridges over one AVA_HOME corrupt each other's data.
AVA_HOME=./ava-data-staging

# Own image tag. docker-compose.yml uses `image:` as BOTH the build output tag
# and the run reference, and both stacks default to ava/bridge:latest — so a slot
# built without this line retags the image the stable stack restarts into. The
# running container keeps serving from its old image ID, so nothing looks wrong
# until stable is next restarted. This is precisely the accident a slot exists to
# prevent.
AVA_IMAGE=ava/bridge:staging

# Own host port. The container still listens on 8096; only the published side
# moves (see slot.yml). Reach the slot at http://127.0.0.1:8097 — NOT
# http://localhost:8097. See the cookie warning below; it is a rule, not a taste.
AVA_PORT_HOST=8097

# No engine containers. The slot joins the stable stack's network and uses its
# already-running engine, so AVA_BACKEND_* below stay exactly as stable has them.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=

# The slot may observe the shared engine's residency but must never unload from
# it — otherwise "Memory Ava can free", which renders on every screen, evicts
# production's model.
AVA_ALLOC_INFER=0

# ── copied from stable by `slot.sh init` ─────────────────────────────────────
# Shown here so the file reads as a whole. AVA_MODEL is required even though no
# vLLM container ever starts in a slot: compose interpolates EVERY service at
# config time regardless of which profiles are selected, and docker-compose.yml
# guards it with ${AVA_MODEL:?...}.
AVA_BACKEND_URL=http://vllm:8002/v1
AVA_BACKEND_ENGINE=vllm
AVA_MODEL=

# ── optional ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Set a password to skip the first-run claim token entirely. Worth doing for a
# throwaway slot: an unclaimed instance is a standing unauthenticated /setup on
# localhost, and the claim link the bridge prints names the IN-CONTAINER port
# (8096), which sends you to the stable slot. `./slot.sh claim` prints the
# correct link if you leave this empty.
AVA_PASSWORD=

# Labels the slot on every screen, the sign-in page, and Setup → System → About.
#
# CAVEAT, and it is not a small one: AVA_NAME is not only a display string. It is
# injected into the SYSTEM PROMPT (ava_bridge/config.py → turns.py, coder.py,
# agent/render_persona.py), so the model is told it is "Ava (staging)" and will
# say so. For UI and API work that is a fine trade. COMMENT THIS OUT whenever the
# slot is testing persona, prompt or agent behaviour — the URL in the address bar
# and the +stg version stamp identify the slot without touching the prompt.
AVA_NAME=Ava (staging)
